            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cd_obj_1",
                    title: "Determine business strategies, goals, initiatives, and challenges to define the scope of a Salesforce project or business initiative.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_1", number: 1, question: "Distinguishing between a business goal, a business initiative, and a project scope when a sponsor presents a vague problem statement", answer: "A business goal is the high-level desired outcome (e.g., 'increase revenue by 15%'). A business initiative is the planned effort or program to achieve that goal (e.g., 'implement a CRM to improve sales tracking'). Project scope is the specific boundary of what will and will not be delivered in a given engagement (e.g., 'deploy Sales Cloud for the North America team with Opportunity management and reporting'). When a sponsor is vague, a BA's job is to ask clarifying questions to peel back the layers: first understand the goal (why), then the initiative (what broadly), then define scope (what specifically within this project)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_2", number: 2, question: "How a BA identifies and documents the business goals and strategic drivers at the start of a Salesforce engagement to define project scope", answer: "At project kickoff, the BA conducts discovery interviews and workshops with executive sponsors and key stakeholders. They ask questions like 'What does success look like in 12 months?' and 'What business problem are we solving?' The BA then documents findings in a project charter or business case, capturing: stated goals, measurable success criteria (KPIs), strategic drivers (e.g., competitive pressure, regulatory change), and how Salesforce will support each driver. This document becomes the anchor for all subsequent scope decisions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_3", number: 3, question: "Techniques for uncovering unstated business challenges during initial discovery conversations with executives", answer: "Executives often describe symptoms, not root causes. BAs use open-ended questions ('What keeps you up at night about this process?'), active listening, and the '5 Whys' technique to drill deeper. Other tactics include reviewing existing reports or dashboards before the meeting to identify performance gaps, asking about past failed initiatives, and probing for pain points in handoffs between teams. The goal is to surface hidden challenges before they become late-stage surprises."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_4", number: 4, question: "How a BA documents a business case or project charter to capture objectives, success criteria, assumptions, and constraints for sponsor approval", answer: "A project charter typically includes: (1) Problem/Opportunity statement, (2) Business objectives with measurable KPIs, (3) Proposed solution overview, (4) In-scope and out-of-scope items, (5) Assumptions (what we believe to be true), (6) Constraints (fixed limitations like budget, timeline, or technology), (7) Stakeholders and roles, and (8) High-level milestones. The BA drafts this and walks sponsors through it for formal sign-off, establishing a shared baseline before work begins."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_5", number: 5, question: "Identifying and documenting project assumptions and constraints during discovery and why they must be validated with stakeholders", answer: "Assumptions are conditions believed to be true but not confirmed (e.g., 'all users have Salesforce licenses'). Constraints are fixed limitations that cannot be changed (e.g., 'go-live must be before Q4 close'). Both must be explicitly documented because unvalidated assumptions become risks—if the assumption turns out to be false, it can derail the project. Stakeholder validation ensures everyone agrees on the boundaries before design begins, preventing costly rework later."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_6", number: 6, question: "How a BA uses SWOT analysis or similar frameworks to align Salesforce project scope with business strategy", answer: "A SWOT analysis (Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats) helps a BA evaluate whether a proposed Salesforce solution fits the business context. Strengths and opportunities inform what the solution should leverage; weaknesses and threats highlight risks and constraints that should shape scope. For example, if a weakness is poor data quality, the BA would include a data cleansing workstream in scope. SWOT ensures the Salesforce project addresses real strategic priorities rather than just solving a surface-level symptom."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_1_7", number: 7, question: "Recognizing when a stated project objective is misaligned with the underlying business strategy and how a BA should respond", answer: "Misalignment occurs when a tactical objective does not actually advance the company's strategic goals (e.g., automating a process the business plans to eliminate). The BA should flag the misalignment by revisiting the business strategy with the sponsor, presenting evidence of the disconnect, and facilitating a discussion to either realign the objective or formally document that the project is intentionally tactical. The BA should not silently proceed with a misaligned scope.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cd_obj_2",
                    title: "Discover how a customer is leveraging existing products to establish the current state.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_2_1", number: 1, question: "Why establishing current state is a prerequisite before defining future state requirements in a Salesforce engagement", answer: "Without understanding the current state, requirements may duplicate existing functionality, ignore technical debt, or miss critical dependencies. The current state baseline reveals what is working (keep), what is broken (fix), and what is missing (add). It also prevents over-engineering—if a feature already exists in Salesforce, it does not need to be rebuilt. Skipping current state analysis is a common cause of scope creep, as undocumented existing processes surface during UAT as 'new requirements.'"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_2_2", number: 2, question: "Approaches a BA uses to document the current state of a customer's Salesforce implementation before recommending changes", answer: "Key approaches include: (1) Org walk-through with an admin to review existing configuration, (2) Process observation (shadowing users in their daily workflows), (3) Reviewing existing reports, dashboards, and data models, (4) Interviewing end users and power users about their actual usage vs. intended usage, and (5) Reviewing the org's setup audit trail or change history. The BA documents findings in process maps, data dictionaries, and system inventory lists."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_2_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA documents existing integrations, data flows, and customizations as part of current-state discovery", answer: "The BA creates an integration inventory that maps each connected system, the direction of data flow (inbound/outbound), frequency, and the business purpose. Customizations (Apex triggers, Flows, validation rules, custom objects) are cataloged with their business rationale. Data flows are diagrammed to show how records move between systems. This documentation is critical for assessing the impact of proposed changes and preventing integration failures during deployment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_2_4", number: 4, question: "Identifying gaps between the customer's current Salesforce usage and best practices during discovery", answer: "The BA compares the customer's actual configuration and usage patterns against Salesforce's recommended best practices (e.g., using Process Builder when Flow is preferred, storing sensitive data in non-encrypted fields, or maintaining excessive custom code where declarative tools would suffice). Gaps are documented and presented as opportunities, not criticisms. This informs recommendations for the future state and helps prioritize technical debt reduction alongside new functionality.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cd_obj_3",
                    title: "Understand and explain Business Analyst roles and responsibilities in different phases of the implementation lifecycle.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_3_1", number: 1, question: "Distinguishing the BA's role from the project manager's and developer's roles during a Salesforce implementation", answer: "The BA focuses on the 'what' and 'why': eliciting, documenting, and validating business requirements. The Project Manager focuses on 'when' and 'how much': scheduling, budgeting, risk management, and delivery coordination. The Developer focuses on 'how': building the technical solution in Salesforce. In practice, the BA is the bridge—translating business needs into requirements the developer can build, and translating technical decisions back into business language for the stakeholders."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_3_2", number: 2, question: "The primary responsibilities of a BA during the requirements-gathering phase versus the build phase of a Salesforce project", answer: "During requirements gathering: eliciting requirements through interviews and workshops, documenting them in requirement specs or user stories, facilitating stakeholder review sessions, and achieving sign-off. During the build phase: answering developer questions about ambiguous requirements, reviewing work-in-progress demos, tracking defects vs. change requests, and managing any scope change requests through a formal process. The BA shifts from a discovery mode to a support and governance mode during build."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_3_3", number: 3, question: "How the BA role shifts from discovery to design to testing across implementation lifecycle phases", answer: "In discovery, the BA leads: facilitating sessions, documenting current state, and capturing requirements. In design, the BA supports: validating proposed solutions against requirements, reviewing wireframes/prototypes, and ensuring nothing is missed. In testing (UAT), the BA leads again: writing test scenarios, coordinating testers, facilitating sessions, and making go/no-go recommendations. The BA is always the guardian of requirements fidelity—ensuring the built solution matches what was agreed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_3_4", number: 4, question: "The BA's role within an Agile/Scrum team: backlog grooming, sprint ceremonies, and iterative requirements refinement", answer: "In an Agile/Scrum context, the BA often collaborates closely with or acts as the Product Owner. Key responsibilities include: maintaining and prioritizing the product backlog, writing and refining user stories before sprint planning (grooming), participating in sprint planning to clarify story details, being available during the sprint to answer developer questions, reviewing demos at sprint review against acceptance criteria, and incorporating feedback into future stories during retrospectives."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_3_5", number: 5, question: "Identifying which BA activities are most critical during the deployment and post-go-live phases", answer: "During deployment: validating that all configurations are complete and match approved requirements, supporting UAT sign-off, contributing to training materials, and confirming data migration accuracy. Post-go-live: monitoring adoption metrics, capturing user feedback for the next iteration, documenting lessons learned, and supporting hypercare (the period immediately after go-live when users need extra support). These activities ensure the solution delivers the expected business value.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cd_obj_4",
                    title: "Apply the implementation lifecycle for planning business analysis activities.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_4_1", number: 1, question: "Sequencing BA activities (discovery, requirements, user stories, UAT) within a standard Salesforce implementation lifecycle", answer: "The standard sequence is: (1) Discovery — understand business goals and current state, (2) Requirements — elicit, document, validate, and baseline requirements, (3) User Stories — decompose requirements into sprint-ready stories with acceptance criteria, (4) Build — support developers, review demos, manage change requests, (5) UAT — coordinate testing with business users, track defects, obtain sign-off, (6) Deployment — validate and support go-live, (7) Post-go-live — monitor adoption and capture feedback. Each phase has BA deliverables that feed the next."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_4_2", number: 2, question: "How a BA plans their own work deliverables, timelines, and stakeholder engagement schedule at the start of an engagement", answer: "The BA creates a Business Analysis Plan that lists all BA deliverables (process maps, requirements doc, user stories, UAT scripts), maps each to a project milestone, and identifies which stakeholders are needed for each activity. The plan includes a stakeholder engagement schedule showing who needs to be available, when, and for how long. This is reviewed with the PM to ensure BA activities align with the overall project timeline and resource availability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_4_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA creates a business analysis plan that aligns BA deliverables to project milestones", answer: "A Business Analysis Plan documents: the scope of BA work, the methods to be used (workshops, interviews, etc.), the deliverables and their owners, the timeline mapped to project milestones, and how changes will be managed. For example, requirements sign-off is mapped to the milestone before build begins. This plan is reviewed and agreed with the PM and key stakeholders to set expectations about BA capacity and sequencing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_4_4", number: 4, question: "Identifying which phase of the implementation lifecycle is the correct point to baseline requirements and freeze scope", answer: "Requirements should be baselined (formally approved and version-controlled) at the end of the requirements phase, immediately before the build phase begins. At this point, all stakeholders have reviewed and signed off on the documented requirements. After baselining, any new or changed requirements must go through a formal change control process. Freezing scope too early (before adequate discovery) risks missing requirements; freezing too late means rework costs accumulate during build."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_4_5", number: 5, question: "Adjusting BA activities when a project switches from a waterfall to an agile delivery approach mid-engagement", answer: "When switching to agile mid-project, the BA shifts from producing comprehensive upfront documentation to writing iterative, sprint-sized user stories. Existing requirements are decomposed into a prioritized product backlog. The BA moves from milestone-driven sign-off cycles to continuous stakeholder engagement through sprint ceremonies. Formal change control is replaced by backlog reprioritization. The BA must help stakeholders understand that agile trades upfront certainty for flexibility and faster delivery of working software.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cd_obj_5",
                    title: "Analyze the customer's Salesforce environment(s) to identify opportunities and constraints.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_5_1", number: 1, question: "How a BA identifies technical constraints in a customer's environment that limit solution options", answer: "The BA works with the admin or architect to review: Salesforce edition (which features are available), governor limits (API call limits, storage, record limits), existing customizations that may conflict with proposed changes, active integrations that cannot be disrupted, and org-wide settings (sharing model, multi-currency, etc.). These constraints become inputs to solution design—they define the guardrails within which the future state must be built. The BA documents them and surfaces them during requirements discussions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_5_2", number: 2, question: "Techniques for analyzing org limits, technical debt, and configuration constraints as inputs to scoping decisions", answer: "Techniques include: reviewing Salesforce Setup > System Overview for storage and API usage; auditing custom objects, fields, and relationships for complexity; reviewing open issues in the org's change/bug log; running a Salesforce Health Check or using tools like Salesforce Optimizer. Technical debt findings (e.g., obsolete workflows, redundant custom fields) inform the BA when proposing scope—some items may need remediation before new features can be added cleanly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_5_3", number: 3, question: "Balancing business opportunity identification with realistic constraints when presenting analysis findings to stakeholders", answer: "The BA presents a balanced view: here is what the business could gain (opportunities), and here is what limits us (constraints). Framing matters—constraints should not be presented as blockers but as inputs to prioritization. For example, 'We cannot implement Feature X in Phase 1 due to integration dependencies, but it is a strong candidate for Phase 2.' This helps stakeholders make informed scope decisions without feeling like the project is being held hostage by technical issues.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cd_obj_6",
                    title: "Demonstrate knowledge of Salesforce capabilities, align them with business needs, and recommend potential solutions.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_6_1", number: 1, question: "Understanding the Salesforce standard data model (standard objects, record relationships, fields) at a level sufficient to assess solution feasibility during discovery", answer: "A BA needs to know that Salesforce organizes data into objects (like Accounts, Contacts, Opportunities, Cases) with standard fields and configurable relationships. Understanding that Accounts are parent to Contacts (lookup) and Opportunities (master-detail) helps the BA assess if a business requirement fits the standard model or needs customization. This knowledge lets the BA have credible conversations with admins and architects about feasibility without needing to know how to configure Salesforce directly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_6_2", number: 2, question: "Mapping a stated business need to an appropriate Salesforce capability category without over-prescribing a specific configuration", answer: "When a stakeholder says 'We need to track follow-up tasks,' the BA maps this to the Activities/Tasks capability in Salesforce rather than immediately recommending a specific Flow or custom solution. The BA operates at the capability level (Sales Cloud, Service Cloud, Marketing Cloud, automation tools, analytics) rather than the configuration level. Over-prescribing a specific technical approach is the architect/developer's job. The BA's role is to confirm that Salesforce has a capability that can meet the need."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_6_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA evaluates whether a business requirement should be met with out-of-the-box Salesforce functionality versus custom development", answer: "The BA applies a 'declarative first' principle: can the requirement be met using standard Salesforce features (fields, page layouts, flows, validation rules, reports)? If yes, custom development adds unnecessary cost and maintenance risk. The BA evaluates by asking: Does standard functionality meet 80%+ of the need? Is the gap truly a business requirement or a preference for a specific UI? Is the workaround reasonable? Only when declarative tools genuinely cannot meet the requirement should custom code be recommended."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_6_4", number: 4, question: "How a BA positions Salesforce capabilities as potential solutions while deferring the final technical design to the architect or developer", answer: "The BA says things like, 'Salesforce has automation tools like Flow that could potentially handle this approval process—let's explore that with the team.' This positions a capability without locking in a design. The BA avoids saying 'We will use a trigger for this' or prescribing specific APIs. After capabilities are confirmed as viable, the architect decides the technical approach. This division of responsibility prevents the BA from inadvertently constraining the technical team's design options."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cd_6_5", number: 5, question: "Recommending a phased Salesforce solution when the business need exceeds the project's current scope or budget", answer: "When the full business need cannot be delivered in one project, the BA works with stakeholders to define phases based on business value and dependency order. Phase 1 delivers the must-have capabilities (MoSCoW: Must Have), Phase 2 delivers important but deferrable items (Should Have), and Phase 3 addresses nice-to-haves. The BA documents the phasing rationale and ensures Phase 1 is designed with future phases in mind to avoid costly re-work when the solution is extended.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Section 2: Collaboration with Stakeholders (38 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "collaboration_with_stakeholders",
            title: "Collaboration with Stakeholders",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.27, blue: 0.68),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.95, green: 0.92, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cs_obj_1",
                    title: "Plan discovery activities based on the target stakeholders to perform a thorough analysis of business needs.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_1_1", number: 1, question: "Creating a discovery plan that sequences stakeholder engagements to build a complete picture of business needs", answer: "A discovery plan sequences sessions strategically: start with executive sponsors to understand goals and priorities, then move to process owners for operational detail, then to end users for ground-level insight. Starting top-down ensures the BA understands strategic context before diving into details, which helps frame user-level conversations. The plan documents each session's objective, format, duration, participants, and pre-reading materials."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_1_2", number: 2, question: "Identifying the right mix of discovery activities (surveys, interviews, job shadowing) for a complex multi-department rollout", answer: "For a multi-department rollout, the BA typically uses: surveys to gather broad input quickly from a large audience, interviews for deep dives with process owners and power users, workshops where cross-departmental alignment is needed, and job shadowing to observe actual workflows that stakeholders struggle to describe verbally. The mix depends on time constraints, stakeholder availability, and whether consensus-building or information-gathering is the primary objective."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_1_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA tailors discovery session formats (workshop, interview, observation) based on stakeholder role and availability", answer: "Executives: short (30-45 min), structured interviews focused on strategy and success criteria—they have limited time and want big-picture conversations. Process owners: workshops or longer interviews to map current-state processes. End users: observation/shadowing is most effective since they often struggle to articulate their process verbally. Remote or unavailable stakeholders: async surveys or document review as a substitute. The format should minimize friction for the stakeholder while maximizing the quality of information captured."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_1_4", number: 4, question: "How a BA prepares targeted questions for a discovery session based on the stakeholder's organizational level", answer: "Executive-level questions focus on strategy ('What does success look like? What are the top three business priorities this initiative must support?'). Manager-level questions focus on process and pain points ('Where does the current process break down? What reports do you wish you had?'). End-user questions focus on day-to-day tasks ('Walk me through what you do when a new lead comes in. What's the most frustrating part of your current tools?'). Tailoring prevents awkward mismatches where a VP is asked about data entry steps.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cs_obj_2",
                    title: "Identify key stakeholders and develop relationships with them as a trusted advisor to act as a liaison between business and technical teams.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_1", number: 1, question: "Techniques for identifying all relevant stakeholders including hidden or indirect stakeholders at the start of an engagement", answer: "Start with a stakeholder identification session with the project sponsor. Ask: 'Who else is affected by this process? Who provides inputs? Who consumes outputs? Who has regulatory or compliance involvement?' Review org charts, process documentation, and existing system user lists. Hidden stakeholders include IT security (for data access), finance (for reporting needs), and downstream teams who receive data from the system being implemented. Missing a key stakeholder early is a leading cause of late-stage requirement changes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_2", number: 2, question: "The BA's role as a liaison translating business needs into technical language for developers and technical findings into business language for sponsors", answer: "When a business user says 'I need to see all my customers who haven't been contacted in 90 days,' the BA translates this into a technical requirement: 'A list view on the Account object filtered by Last Activity Date > 90 days ago, accessible to Sales users.' Conversely, when a developer says 'We need to respect governor limits on SOQL queries,' the BA translates: 'There's a system constraint that limits how many searches run at once—we need to restructure the report to work within those limits.' This translation prevents miscommunication."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_3", number: 3, question: "Using a stakeholder map to identify engagement strategies for each stakeholder group", answer: "A stakeholder map is a visual diagram showing all stakeholders positioned by their relationship to the project (internal/external, by department, by level). For each group, the BA documents: what they care about, their communication preferences, their level of Salesforce knowledge, and the appropriate engagement strategy. For example, end users may need hands-on demos, while executives need concise summary updates. The map ensures no stakeholder group is forgotten or under-engaged."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_4", number: 4, question: "Using the Power/Interest Grid to categorize stakeholders and determine the appropriate level of engagement for each category (manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed, monitor)", answer: "The Power/Interest Grid plots stakeholders on two axes: Power (influence over the project) and Interest (degree of impact on them). High Power + High Interest: Manage Closely (frequent, detailed engagement—typically sponsors and key process owners). High Power + Low Interest: Keep Satisfied (periodic updates to maintain goodwill—e.g., senior executives not directly involved). Low Power + High Interest: Keep Informed (regular updates—e.g., end users). Low Power + Low Interest: Monitor (minimal engagement—e.g., peripheral departments). This prevents over-investing in low-impact relationships while under-serving critical ones."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_5", number: 5, question: "Using a RACI matrix to identify engagement strategies for each stakeholder group", answer: "RACI stands for Responsible (does the work), Accountable (owns the outcome), Consulted (provides input), Informed (receives updates). For BA activities: the BA is typically Responsible for requirements documentation; the business sponsor is Accountable for approving them; subject matter experts are Consulted; and the development team is Informed. RACI prevents engagement gaps (key people not consulted) and overload (too many people in the 'Responsible' column creating confusion)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_6", number: 6, question: "What the DACI framework roles mean (Driver, Approver, Contributor, Informed) in decision-making", answer: "DACI is a decision-making framework: Driver — the person who drives the decision process forward (typically the BA or PM), ensures the decision gets made. Approver — the single person with final decision authority (a sponsor or senior leader). Contributor — people who provide input and expertise to inform the decision (SMEs, developers). Informed — people who need to know the outcome but do not shape it. Unlike RACI, DACI is decision-specific—each major project decision has its own DACI assignment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_7", number: 7, question: "Applying the DACI framework to clarify decision-making roles and resolve stakeholder conflicts on a Salesforce project", answer: "When stakeholders from two departments disagree on a requirement, the BA applies DACI: Who is the Approver for this decision? Is it the VP of Sales or the VP of Service? Once the Approver is identified and agreed, the BA facilitates input from Contributors, documents the decision with rationale, and informs everyone else. This removes the ambiguity of 'who decides' that often stalls projects. The BA acts as the Driver—not making the decision but ensuring it gets made with the right information."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_8", number: 8, question: "Creating a stakeholder communication plan that defines what information is communicated to whom, at what frequency, and through what channel", answer: "A stakeholder communication plan is a table/matrix that lists: each stakeholder group, the type of information they need (project status, milestone updates, requirement reviews), the frequency (weekly, bi-weekly, at key milestones), the channel (email, Slack, steering committee meeting, SharePoint), and the sender/owner. It prevents information gaps and ensures stakeholders are not surprised by decisions. The plan is reviewed and agreed at project kickoff and updated when stakeholder needs change."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_9", number: 9, question: "How a BA adapts communication style and content depth when presenting to executive sponsors versus end users versus technical developers", answer: "Executives: high-level, outcome-focused, use business metrics and risk language, limit to 5-10 minutes with a one-page summary. End users: concrete, scenario-based, show what it means for their daily tasks—use demos or visual mockups rather than text documents. Technical developers: detailed, precise, include data model specifics, field-level requirements, and edge cases. The same information must be packaged differently for each audience. A BA who pitches a developer's level of detail to an executive—or a high-level vision to a developer—loses credibility with both."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_10", number: 10, question: "How a BA builds credibility and trust with a skeptical senior stakeholder who resists the Salesforce initiative", answer: "Start by listening—understand why they are skeptical (past failed projects, loss of control, fear of change). Acknowledge their concerns genuinely. Bring evidence: data showing the problem that Salesforce will solve, and references to similar successful implementations. Involve them early: make them a Contributor in DACI so they feel ownership. Deliver on small commitments quickly to demonstrate reliability. Avoid overselling; be honest about limitations. Trust is earned through consistent honesty and follow-through, not by winning arguments."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_2_11", number: 11, question: "Recognizing when a BA should escalate a stakeholder conflict versus resolving it directly within the team", answer: "A BA should attempt direct resolution when: the conflict involves peer-level stakeholders, the disagreement is about requirements details rather than strategic priorities, and both parties are willing to collaborate. Escalation is appropriate when: the conflict involves competing priorities that require executive decision-making authority, a stakeholder is blocking progress and is not responding to facilitation, or the decision has significant budget or timeline implications. Escalate to the project sponsor or steering committee with a clear summary of the options and their trade-offs, not just the problem.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cs_obj_3",
                    title: "Given a scenario, choose the most effective technique for eliciting business needs from stakeholders.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_1", number: 1, question: "Choosing between interviews, workshops, observation, prototyping, and document analysis based on the elicitation goal and stakeholder context", answer: "Interviews: best for deep-dive with individuals, sensitive topics, or executives. Workshops: best when cross-functional alignment or group consensus is needed. Observation/shadowing: best when stakeholders cannot articulate their process verbally. Prototyping: best when stakeholders struggle to visualize requirements in abstract—showing a UI prompt more specific feedback. Document analysis: best when SMEs are unavailable or when existing materials (SOPs, reports) can substitute. Match the technique to the primary goal: information gathering (interviews/observation) vs. consensus building (workshops) vs. requirement validation (prototyping)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_2", number: 2, question: "The difference between open-ended and closed-ended questions and when to use each during requirements elicitation", answer: "Open-ended questions invite narrative responses and surface unexpected information ('Tell me about your current process for handling customer complaints'). Use them early in discovery to explore and learn. Closed-ended questions seek confirmation or specific data ('Does the system currently send an email when a case is closed?'). Use them to validate specifics or confirm understanding. A good elicitation session uses open-ended questions to explore, then closes with targeted questions to confirm what was heard."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_3", number: 3, question: "When facilitated workshops are preferable to one-on-one interviews for requirements elicitation", answer: "Workshops are preferable when: multiple stakeholders need to align on a shared process, conflicting requirements from different teams need to be resolved in real time, brainstorming benefits from group energy and cross-pollination of ideas, or time is limited and parallel input is needed. Interviews are better when: topics are sensitive or political, stakeholders need individual preparation time, or stakeholder seniority makes group dynamics awkward. Workshops save time but require skilled facilitation to prevent dominance by vocal participants."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_4", number: 4, question: "Timeboxing agenda items in workshops to achieve elicitation objectives", answer: "Timeboxing assigns a fixed time limit to each agenda item (e.g., 20 minutes to map the lead-to-opportunity process, 15 minutes for Q&A). It prevents one topic from consuming the entire session, ensures all agenda items are addressed, and creates a sense of urgency that keeps discussions focused. The BA communicates timeboxes upfront, uses a visible timer, and enforces them by moving off-topic items to a parking lot rather than letting discussions run over."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_5", number: 5, question: "Keeping workshop discussions on track to achieve elicitation objectives", answer: "Techniques include: stating the session objective clearly at the start ('Today's goal is to document the case escalation process'), referring back to the agenda when discussions drift, using a parking lot for off-topic but valid items, asking redirecting questions ('How does that relate to the process step we are mapping?'), and involving participants by calling on quieter members to balance dominant voices. The BA is both facilitator and timekeeper—quality outcomes require active session management."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_6", number: 6, question: "Managing a parking lot during facilitated workshops to capture off-topic items", answer: "A parking lot is a visible list (whiteboard, shared doc, or virtual sticky note board) where the BA captures valid but off-topic discussion items without letting them derail the session. When an item is parked, the BA acknowledges its value ('That's important—let me put it in the parking lot so we make sure it gets addressed'), preventing the contributor from feeling dismissed. At the end of the session, the BA reviews all parked items and assigns owners or schedules follow-up sessions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_7", number: 7, question: "Using brainstorming to surface requirements from a large diverse stakeholder group", answer: "In a brainstorming session, all participants generate ideas freely without judgment. The BA facilitates with a clear prompt ('What capabilities does the new system need to support your team?'), collects responses rapidly (sticky notes, digital whiteboard, round-robin verbal sharing), and defers evaluation until the generation phase is complete. This prevents groupthink, surfaces diverse perspectives, and ensures quieter participants contribute. After generation, the BA groups and prioritizes the ideas with the group."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_8", number: 8, question: "Using affinity mapping to surface requirements from a large diverse stakeholder group", answer: "Affinity mapping (affinity diagramming) is used to organize a large volume of individual ideas into themed clusters. After a brainstorming session, the BA works with participants to group related sticky notes/ideas into categories (e.g., 'Reporting needs,' 'Automation needs,' 'Integration needs'). These clusters become natural requirement categories. Affinity mapping is especially useful when a diverse group has generated many disparate ideas that need to be structured before they can be prioritized or assigned."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_9", number: 9, question: "Using prototypes or wireframes as an elicitation tool to prompt more specific requirements from non-technical stakeholders", answer: "Abstract requirements ('We need a better dashboard') become concrete when a stakeholder sees a wireframe or Salesforce prototype. Seeing a visual representation triggers reactions ('This is close, but I also need to see the contract value' or 'This should only be visible to managers'). The prototype does not need to be fully functional—even a basic mockup in tools like Figma or a Salesforce sandbox drives much richer requirements conversations than blank-page interviews. It is especially effective with stakeholders who think visually."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_10", number: 10, question: "Selecting observation (shadowing) as the most appropriate elicitation technique when stakeholders cannot articulate their own processes", answer: "Many knowledge workers struggle to describe their own processes accurately because they have become automatic routines. Shadowing involves the BA sitting alongside a user during their actual work and observing in real time. The BA notes each step, tool used, decision made, and workaround employed. This surfaces the 'hidden' process that interviews miss. It is particularly valuable for call center agents, field reps, or data entry roles where the work is highly procedural and difficult to describe abstractly."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_3_11", number: 11, question: "Using document analysis (reviewing existing reports, SOPs, and process guides) as an elicitation technique when subject-matter experts are unavailable", answer: "Document analysis means reviewing existing artifacts—standard operating procedures, training manuals, existing system reports, org charts, data dictionaries, and meeting minutes—to extract requirements without needing live stakeholder time. It is ideal when SMEs are constrained or when the BA needs to pre-populate process maps before a workshop. Limitations: documents may be outdated or incomplete. The BA should validate document-derived requirements with a stakeholder before finalizing them.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cs_obj_4",
                    title: "Explain how to move from current state analysis to an agreed future state design.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_4_1", number: 1, question: "How a BA uses current-state process maps as a baseline for facilitating agreement on a future-state design", answer: "The BA presents the validated current-state process map to stakeholders and asks: 'Where does this process work well? Where does it break down?' Each identified pain point becomes a target for improvement in the future state. The BA facilitates a structured conversation where stakeholders propose improvements step by step, ensuring the future state is grounded in real problems rather than theoretical ideals. The current-state map prevents the group from designing a future state that forgets how things actually work today."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_4_2", number: 2, question: "Facilitating a gap analysis workshop to help stakeholders identify the delta between current state and desired future state", answer: "A gap analysis workshop places the current-state process map and the desired future-state vision side by side. The BA facilitates a comparison: what steps exist now that will be eliminated, changed, or kept? What new steps or capabilities are needed? Each gap is documented as a requirement or an issue that must be resolved. The output is a gap register that feeds directly into requirements documentation. This structured approach prevents the future state from being designed in isolation from current reality."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_4_3", number: 3, question: "Steps a BA follows to achieve stakeholder sign-off on the future state design before requirements are written", answer: "Steps: (1) Draft the future-state process map based on workshop outputs, (2) Share with participants for async review (typically 3-5 business days), (3) Incorporate feedback and version the document, (4) Schedule a review session to walk through the updated draft and resolve outstanding questions, (5) Obtain formal sign-off (email confirmation or signature on the document). Sign-off before requirements writing ensures that requirements are anchored to an agreed future state, not a moving target."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_4_4", number: 4, question: "Managing stakeholder disagreement about what the future state should look like and driving toward consensus", answer: "When stakeholders disagree, the BA first ensures both parties have heard each other's position by summarizing each view neutrally. Then the BA brings the discussion back to agreed business goals: 'Which option better supports our goal of reducing case resolution time?' If the disagreement cannot be resolved at the working level, the BA applies DACI to identify the Approver and escalates with a clear summary of both options and their trade-offs. The BA documents the outcome in a decision log regardless of which path is chosen.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "cs_obj_5",
                    title: "Given a scenario, manage competing priorities to obtain stakeholder consensus.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_1", number: 1, question: "Techniques for facilitating consensus when stakeholders from different departments have conflicting requirements", answer: "Key techniques: (1) Bring stakeholders together in a joint session rather than managing conflicts bilaterally, (2) Anchor discussions to shared business goals ('We all agree the goal is to reduce time-to-close—which approach supports that better?'), (3) Use structured prioritization tools (MoSCoW, dot-voting) to make preferences explicit and objective, (4) Separate the people from the problem—frame disagreements as 'two valid options' rather than 'your view vs. their view,' (5) Document the agreed outcome in a decision log to prevent re-opening settled debates."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_2", number: 2, question: "How a BA uses MoSCoW prioritization to help a group of stakeholders prioritize a backlog of competing requirements", answer: "MoSCoW categorizes requirements as: Must Have (non-negotiable; the project fails without it), Should Have (important but could be deferred), Could Have (nice to have if time/budget allows), Won't Have this time (explicitly excluded for now). The BA facilitates the group through each requirement, asking 'If we could only deliver this one thing, would the project still have business value?' to test Must Have claims. MoSCoW creates a shared language for trade-offs and prevents every stakeholder from labeling their item as 'critical.'"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA uses dot-voting to help a group of stakeholders prioritize competing requirements", answer: "Dot-voting (also called multi-voting) gives each participant a fixed number of votes (e.g., 5 dots) and asks them to place their votes on the requirements they feel are most important. Participants can split votes or stack them on one item. The BA counts votes and ranks requirements by total votes received. This technique is fast, visual, democratic, and surfaces genuine group preferences rather than allowing the loudest voice to dominate. It works best after requirements have been listed and briefly explained to all voters."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_4", number: 4, question: "What a RAID log tracks: Risks, Assumptions, Issues, and Dependencies", answer: "RAID log: Risks — potential future events that could negatively impact the project (with probability and impact ratings). Assumptions — conditions believed to be true; if they turn out false, they become risks or issues. Issues — problems that have already occurred and need resolution. Dependencies — tasks, decisions, or deliverables that must be completed before another can proceed. The RAID log is a living document maintained throughout the project. The BA contributes to it by surfacing risks and assumptions during discovery and requirements sessions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_5", number: 5, question: "How a BA uses a RAID log to track and communicate project-level concerns that affect stakeholder decisions", answer: "The BA reviews the RAID log regularly (typically in sync with sprint cadence or weekly status meetings) and surfaces items that require stakeholder decisions: 'We have an assumption that the legacy system will be decommissioned before go-live—we need to confirm this with IT or it becomes a dependency risk.' Bringing RAID items to stakeholders proactively ensures decisions are made before they become critical blockers. The log also provides an audit trail of what was known, when, and what action was taken."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_6", number: 6, question: "Using a decision log to document agreed priorities and prevent scope debates from reopening during later project phases", answer: "A decision log records: the decision made, the date, who approved it, the alternatives considered, and the rationale. When a stakeholder tries to re-open a previously settled scope debate ('But I thought we were going to include the mobile app'), the BA refers to the decision log as the authoritative record. This prevents revisiting decisions already made and keeps the project moving forward. The decision log is distinct from meeting notes—it captures only formal decisions, not discussion."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_7", number: 7, question: "Identifying when competing stakeholder priorities should be escalated to an executive sponsor for resolution", answer: "Escalation to the executive sponsor is appropriate when: the conflict involves budget trade-offs or timeline changes, working-level stakeholders have genuinely irreconcilable priorities, a stakeholder refuses to participate in the consensus process, or the decision has strategic implications that are above the working team's authority level. The BA escalates with a clean summary: 'Here are two options, here are the trade-offs, and here is what we need decided by [date].' Escalating too early undermines the BA's credibility; escalating too late causes project delays."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "cs_5_8", number: 8, question: "Strategies for maintaining project momentum when one key stakeholder repeatedly blocks consensus on requirements", answer: "Strategies: (1) Understand the root cause of resistance—is it fear, workload, or genuine disagreement? Address the root cause. (2) Involve the stakeholder's manager in setting expectations about their participation. (3) Use DACI to formally identify who the Approver is—if the blocker is not the Approver, their veto does not apply. (4) Time-box decisions: inform the stakeholder that if a decision is not received by a specific date, the project will proceed with a default option. (5) Escalate through the project sponsor if repeated blocking creates risk to the timeline.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Section 3: Business Process Mapping (14 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "business_process_mapping",
            title: "Business Process Mapping",
            color: Color(red: 0.17, green: 0.63, blue: 0.45),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.98, blue: 0.93),
            icon: "arrow.triangle.branch",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "bpm_obj_1",
                    title: "Demonstrate how to define the scope of a complex business process and break it down into manageable steps.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_1_1", number: 1, question: "How a BA defines process boundaries (start event, end event, in-scope sub-processes) for a complex cross-functional workflow", answer: "Process boundaries define what is inside and outside the process being mapped. The start event is the trigger that initiates the process (e.g., 'Customer submits a support request'). The end event is the condition that marks the process as complete (e.g., 'Case is closed and customer is notified'). In-scope sub-processes are the intermediate steps included in the analysis. Boundary decisions are made with stakeholders to prevent scope from expanding indefinitely. Clearly stating 'This process begins when X and ends when Y' anchors the mapping effort."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_1_2", number: 2, question: "Decomposing a high-level business process into sub-processes and tasks to a level of detail appropriate for requirements elicitation", answer: "Process decomposition works hierarchically: Level 1 is the high-level process (e.g., Order to Cash). Level 2 breaks it into sub-processes (e.g., Order Entry, Credit Check, Fulfillment, Invoicing, Payment Collection). Level 3 breaks each sub-process into tasks (e.g., Order Entry: Receive order, validate product availability, confirm pricing, create order record). The BA stops decomposing when the level of detail is sufficient to write requirements or user stories. Over-decomposing wastes time; under-decomposing leaves gaps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_1_3", number: 3, question: "Using swim-lane diagrams to delineate responsibilities and handoffs between departments in a business process", answer: "A swim-lane diagram (also called a cross-functional flowchart) assigns each department or role its own horizontal or vertical lane. Process steps are placed in the lane of the responsible party, and arrows crossing lanes represent handoffs. This makes it immediately visible who does what and where delays or miscommunications typically occur (at the handoffs). Swim lanes are especially useful for cross-departmental processes like quote-to-cash or case escalation, where accountability is often unclear."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_1_4", number: 4, question: "Identifying the correct level of process decomposition—knowing when a process map is detailed enough to drive requirements", answer: "A process map is detailed enough when: each task/step can be directly translated into a system requirement or user story, the performer, trigger, input, action, and output of each step are clear, exception paths and decision points are captured, and SMEs agree the map accurately represents the real process. Signs it is too high-level: steps are vague ('process request'), no decision points are shown. Signs it is too detailed: you are documenting keystrokes or menu clicks rather than business-level steps.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "bpm_obj_2",
                    title: "Analyze and document a business process to elicit requirements and engage stakeholders to visualize a future state.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_1", number: 1, question: "Selecting the appropriate process notation (BPMN, flowchart, swim lane, UPN) for communicating a process to different stakeholder audiences", answer: "BPMN (Business Process Model and Notation): formal, symbol-rich notation best for technical teams and architects who need precise process specifications. Flowchart: simple boxes and arrows, universally understood, good for simple linear processes and general audiences. Swim-lane diagram: flowchart with role/department lanes, best for cross-functional processes where accountability matters. UPN (Universal Process Notation): layered, plain-language notation designed for business users—best for non-technical stakeholders. Match the notation to the audience's literacy, not to what the BA prefers."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_2", number: 2, question: "The key characteristics of Universal Process Notation (UPN): who, what, when, why, how; and layered detail levels", answer: "UPN answers five questions for every process box: Who (the role responsible), What (the action performed), When (the trigger or timing), Why (the business reason), and How (the method or tool used). Each process box can be clicked to reveal the next level of detail—creating a layered, drillable view that allows executives to see the high-level view while end users can drill into task-level detail. UPN uses simple plain-language boxes rather than formal symbols, making it accessible to non-technical business users without training."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_3", number: 3, question: "How Universal Process Notation (UPN) differs from BPMN and basic flowcharts", answer: "UPN: designed for business users; uses plain-language activity boxes; supports layered detail (click to expand); answers who/what/when/why/how in each box. BPMN: designed for technical process architects; uses formal symbols (events, gateways, tasks, pools, lanes); supports complex parallel flows and exception handling; requires training to read. Basic flowchart: simple shapes (box, diamond, arrow); no formal standard; easy to read but limited in detail and scalability. Key differentiator: UPN is business-readable and layered; BPMN is technically precise but requires expertise."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_4", number: 4, question: "When to use UPN versus a swimlane diagram versus a value stream map when documenting a Salesforce business process", answer: "UPN: use when the primary audience is business stakeholders who need to understand and validate a process without process modeling training. Swim-lane diagram: use when the key question is 'who does what' and cross-functional handoffs need to be visible—ideal for showing where Salesforce will automate or facilitate a handoff. Value stream map: use when the focus is on identifying waste, delays, and non-value-added steps in an end-to-end process—more common in lean/operations contexts than typical Salesforce BA work."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_5", number: 5, question: "Facilitating a process mapping session with end users to capture the as-is workflow and identify pain points", answer: "Preparation: share a simple process map template and agenda in advance. During the session: start with the trigger ('What starts this process?'), walk through step by step using sticky notes or a digital whiteboard, ask 'What happens next? Who does it?' at each step. Actively probe for pain points: 'Where does this process slow down? What do you do when something goes wrong?' Document exceptions as branches. After the session: clean up and share the draft with participants for review. End users are the most reliable source of as-is process truth."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_6", number: 6, question: "Using a documented as-is process map to derive functional requirements for the to-be Salesforce solution", answer: "The BA walks through the as-is process map and for each step asks: 'Should Salesforce do this? Should it automate this? Should it notify someone? Should it capture data at this point?' Each answer generates a candidate requirement. Pain points in the as-is process become improvement requirements for the to-be solution. Steps that Salesforce should replace with automation become automation requirements. Steps that remain manual but need Salesforce data become reporting or visibility requirements. The as-is map is the seed from which to-be requirements grow."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_7", number: 7, question: "Validating a draft process map with stakeholders to confirm accuracy and surface missed steps or exceptions", answer: "Validation involves sharing the draft map with the stakeholders who participated in mapping, plus any SMEs who were not present. The BA walks through the map step by step in a review session, asking 'Does this accurately reflect how the process works today?' and 'What have we missed?' Common gaps found in validation: exception paths ('What if the customer doesn't respond?'), handoff steps between teams, and system-specific steps the BA did not observe. Validation must occur before the map is used as a requirements baseline."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_2_8", number: 8, question: "Identifying exception paths and edge cases in a business process that are not visible in the happy path", answer: "The happy path is the process flow when everything goes as expected. Exception paths are what happens when something goes wrong or an unusual condition occurs (e.g., 'What if the credit check fails? What if the product is out of stock? What if the customer disputes the invoice?'). BAs uncover exception paths by asking 'What could go wrong here? What do you do when X doesn't work?' during process mapping sessions. Exception paths often generate some of the most important requirements—and are frequently forgotten until UAT.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "bpm_obj_3",
                    title: "Document business processes in a version controlled repository to manage scope.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_3_1", number: 1, question: "Why process documents should be stored in a version-controlled repository and how this supports scope management", answer: "Version control gives every document a history: who changed it, when, and what changed. For scope management, this means: (1) The baseline version (the signed-off version) is always recoverable, (2) Scope changes are traceable—you can see exactly when and why a process step was added or removed, (3) Disputes about what was agreed are resolved by looking at the version that was signed off, not memory. Without version control, process documents become unreliable as they accumulate informal edits and stakeholders disagree about what the 'current' version says."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "bpm_3_2", number: 2, question: "How a BA uses version history of process documents to manage scope changes and communicate decisions", answer: "When a stakeholder requests a process change after sign-off, the BA updates the document with a new version number, documents what changed and why in a change log, and routes the updated version for approval. The version history shows the delta between the baseline and the proposed change, making the impact explicit. This is presented to the stakeholder as: 'Here is the original agreed process, here is the proposed change, and here is the impact on scope and timeline.' The decision and approval are recorded in the change log.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Section 4: Requirements (21 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "requirements",
            title: "Requirements",
            color: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.49, blue: 0.13),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.95, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "req_obj_1",
                    title: "Understand the differences between scope, requirements and user stories.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_1", number: 1, question: "Distinguishing between project scope, a business requirement, a functional requirement, and a user story", answer: "Project scope: the boundaries of the entire engagement—what is included and excluded ('The project covers Sales Cloud for North America'). Business requirement: a statement of a business need or outcome ('Sales managers need visibility into pipeline health'). Functional requirement: a specific capability the system must provide to meet the business requirement ('The system must display a dashboard showing Opportunity stage distribution by owner'). User story: a development-ready description of a feature from the user's perspective ('As a Sales Manager, I want a pipeline dashboard so that I can identify at-risk deals'). Each level is progressively more specific."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_2", number: 2, question: "How scope, requirements, and user stories relate hierarchically and how changes to one affect the others", answer: "Scope defines the outer boundary; requirements describe what must be true within that scope; user stories are the development-ready decomposition of those requirements. A change to scope (expanding or contracting the project) requires reviewing all requirements and user stories to add, remove, or modify items accordingly. A change to a requirement ripples down to the user stories that implement it and potentially up to scope if it represents significantly new work. This hierarchy means changes must be impact-assessed at all levels."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_3", number: 3, question: "Distinguishing between functional requirements (what the system does) and non-functional requirements (how the system performs)", answer: "Functional requirements describe system behavior and capabilities: 'The system must automatically assign a new Lead to the territory manager based on zip code.' Non-functional requirements describe qualities of the system: performance ('The dashboard must load in under 3 seconds'), security ('Only Managers can view salary data'), scalability ('The solution must support 10,000 concurrent users'), usability ('The mobile interface must be navigable with one thumb'). Both types are essential—a functionally correct solution that is too slow or insecure fails to meet business needs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_4", number: 4, question: "The main categories of non-functional requirements: performance, security, scalability, usability, and availability", answer: "Performance: how fast the system responds under expected load. Security: who can access what data, how data is encrypted and audited. Scalability: how the system behaves as data volumes or user counts grow. Usability: how easy and intuitive the interface is for the target users. Availability: what uptime/downtime windows are acceptable (e.g., '99.9% uptime during business hours'). In Salesforce, these often translate to: sharing rules and permission sets (security), Salesforce governor limits (scalability), report load times (performance), and page layout design (usability)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_5", number: 5, question: "Why both functional and non-functional requirements must be captured during elicitation", answer: "Functional requirements without non-functional requirements lead to solutions that work correctly in demo conditions but fail in production. For example, a Salesforce report that returns correct data but takes 45 seconds to load will be abandoned by users. Non-functional requirements set the quality bar that the solution must meet, not just the features it must have. Capturing both types during elicitation—rather than discovering performance or security gaps in UAT—prevents expensive late-stage rework and ensures the delivered solution is truly fit for purpose."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_6", number: 6, question: "Where each category of non-functional requirement surfaces in a Salesforce project", answer: "Performance: large report queries, Flow trigger volumes, API callout frequency. Security: field-level security, sharing rules, profiles/permission sets, encryption at rest. Scalability: data storage limits, API call limits, record-triggered automation volume. Usability: page layout design, mobile optimization, accessibility compliance. Availability: Salesforce's standard SLA is 99.9%—custom integrations or batch jobs may add downtime risk. The BA surfaces these during requirements discussions by probing: 'How many users will use this feature? Who should have access? How quickly do you need results?'"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_7", number: 7, question: "Classifying a given artifact as scope statement, requirement, or user story based on its characteristics", answer: "Scope statement: describes project boundaries, not specific capabilities—'The project will implement Sales Cloud for the North America sales team, including Lead management and Opportunity tracking.' Requirement: describes a specific need or capability—'The system must send an email notification to the account owner when an Opportunity moves to Closed Won.' User story: follows As a / I want / So that format and is small enough for a single sprint—'As an Account Executive, I want to receive an email when my Opportunity closes so that I can immediately follow up with the customer.'"),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_1_8", number: 8, question: "Recognizing when a stakeholder has provided a solution instead of a requirement and how a BA re-frames it", answer: "Solution statement (what to avoid): 'We need a button on the Account page that triggers a Flow to send a Slack message.' Underlying requirement (what the BA should extract): 'Sales reps need to be notified immediately when a key account's status changes.' The BA re-frames by asking 'What business problem does this button solve? What happens if you don't get that Slack message?' This surfaces the real need, which may have multiple valid solutions. Letting stakeholders dictate solutions constrains the design team and can produce suboptimal implementations.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "req_obj_2",
                    title: "Engage stakeholders to identify, validate, and prioritize requirements.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_1", number: 1, question: "Process for validating requirements with stakeholders to confirm accuracy, completeness, and feasibility before development", answer: "Validation process: (1) Share drafted requirements with stakeholders for async review (3-5 days), (2) Hold a requirements walkthrough session where each requirement is read aloud and stakeholders confirm, question, or flag issues, (3) Resolve open issues—clarify ambiguous language, close conflicts between requirements, fill gaps, (4) Obtain formal sign-off from the appropriate stakeholders (typically the sponsor and process owners), (5) Baseline the approved requirements document in version control. Validation catches errors cheaply—finding a gap in requirements is far less expensive than finding it during UAT."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_2", number: 2, question: "Techniques for confirming requirements are testable and unambiguous before handing off to development", answer: "A requirement is testable if you can write a specific test case for it. Test it by asking: 'How would I know if this requirement is met or not met?' If the answer is vague, the requirement is not testable. Techniques: (1) Rewrite vague language—'fast' becomes 'loads in under 3 seconds,' 'easy to use' becomes 'users can complete the task in under 2 minutes without training,' (2) Ensure each requirement has a single, verifiable condition, (3) Walk through the requirement with a developer and QA analyst—if either says 'I'm not sure how to build/test this,' it needs more clarity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_3", number: 3, question: "Conducting a requirements walkthrough or inspection with stakeholders to identify ambiguities, conflicts, and missing requirements before sign-off", answer: "A requirements walkthrough is a structured review where the BA leads stakeholders through each requirement one by one. Participants look for: ambiguities (language that could be interpreted more than one way), conflicts (two requirements that cannot both be true), duplicates (the same need stated twice), and gaps (needs identified in discovery that are missing from the document). The BA documents all findings and resolves them before requesting sign-off. Inspections can be formal (with a structured defect log) or informal (discussion-based)—both are more effective than email review alone."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_4", number: 4, question: "Identifying non-functional requirements (performance, security, usability) during stakeholder engagement", answer: "NFRs rarely surface unless the BA specifically asks for them. Targeted probing questions: 'How many users will use this feature simultaneously?' (scalability), 'Who should not be able to see this data?' (security), 'How quickly do you need results—is a 5-second load time acceptable?' (performance), 'Do any users access Salesforce on mobile?' (usability), 'Are there regulatory requirements about data retention?' (compliance). The BA includes NFR discovery as a formal agenda item in requirements workshops, not an afterthought."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_5", number: 5, question: "Techniques for eliciting requirements from stakeholders who are unavailable or difficult to engage", answer: "Techniques: (1) Document analysis—review their existing SOPs, reports, and email trails to infer their requirements, (2) Proxy interviews—interview a colleague or subordinate who knows their processes, (3) Async methods—send a structured questionnaire that they can complete on their schedule, (4) Observation—shadow the process without needing the stakeholder to narrate, (5) Prototype review—send a mockup and ask for written feedback. The BA should also escalate repeated unavailability to the PM or sponsor, as missing a key stakeholder's requirements is a project risk."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_6", number: 6, question: "How a BA handles conflicting requirements submitted by stakeholders in different parts of the business", answer: "Process: (1) Document both conflicting requirements clearly, (2) Identify the root cause of the conflict—is it different business processes, different data definitions, or simply different priorities? (3) Bring the conflicting parties together to surface the tension and discuss the trade-offs, (4) Apply DACI to determine who has the authority to resolve the conflict, (5) Propose options and facilitate a decision, (6) Document the resolution in the decision log and update requirements to reflect the agreed position. Never silently choose one requirement over the other without stakeholder involvement."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_7", number: 7, question: "Applying MoSCoW prioritization to requirements when a project's scope exceeds available development capacity", answer: "When all requirements cannot be delivered within the project constraints, the BA facilitates a MoSCoW session with stakeholders to categorize each requirement. Must Have items are non-negotiable—the project delivers them or it fails to meet its business objective. Should Have items are important but deliverable in Phase 2 without business failure. Could Have items are enhancements added only if capacity allows. Won't Have items are explicitly deferred. This produces a prioritized requirement list that aligns delivery capacity with business value, preventing the common trap of trying to deliver everything and delivering nothing well."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_8", number: 8, question: "How a requirements traceability matrix links each requirement to its source, its corresponding user story, its test case, and its delivered feature to prove coverage", answer: "A requirements traceability matrix (RTM) is a table where each row is a requirement. Columns include: Requirement ID, Requirement Description, Source (which stakeholder or document it came from), Related User Story IDs, Related Test Case IDs, and Delivery Status (Not started / In development / Complete / Verified). This matrix proves that every requirement has a user story to implement it, a test case to verify it, and tracks whether it has been delivered. Auditors and sponsors use the RTM to confirm complete coverage at go-live."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_9", number: 9, question: "How a BA uses a requirements traceability matrix to track requirements through the project lifecycle", answer: "The RTM is updated throughout the project: during requirements, each requirement is added with its source; during story creation, user story IDs are added; during QA, test case IDs are linked; during UAT, the verified column is checked off. At any point, the BA can use the RTM to identify: requirements without user stories (coverage gap), user stories without test cases (testing gap), or requirements that have been delivered but not yet verified (UAT backlog). It is the BA's primary tool for proving requirements completeness."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_2_10", number: 10, question: "Performing impact analysis when a late-stage change request arrives: determining which existing requirements, user stories, and test cases are affected", answer: "When a change request arrives, the BA uses the RTM to trace the blast radius: which existing requirements does this change modify or invalidate? Which user stories implement those requirements and will need to be rewritten or re-estimated? Which test cases will need to be updated? The BA documents the full impact—scope, effort, timeline, and cost—and presents it to the decision-maker (sponsor or steering committee) before approving the change. This prevents 'innocent-looking' change requests from silently destroying the project timeline.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "req_obj_3",
                    title: "Document requirements in a version controlled repository to manage scope.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_3_1", number: 1, question: "How storing requirements in a version-controlled repository prevents scope creep and preserves decision history", answer: "Version control ensures that the baseline (signed-off) requirements document is always recoverable. When a stakeholder informally requests a change—'Can we just add this one field?'—the BA can point to the baselined document and route the request through a formal change process. Without version control, informal changes accumulate and the requirements document drifts away from what was actually agreed, making it impossible to know what was committed to versus what was added later. The history log also shows who approved each change."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_3_2", number: 2, question: "Best practices for structuring a requirements document to support traceability and change management", answer: "Best practices: (1) Assign a unique ID to each requirement (e.g., REQ-001), (2) Organize by functional area or process, (3) Include version number, date, and author for each document version, (4) Maintain a change log at the beginning of the document listing what changed between versions, (5) Use consistent language and format for each requirement (e.g., 'The system SHALL...'), (6) Separate functional and non-functional requirements into distinct sections, (7) Include a traceability section linking each requirement to its business objective. Structured documents support both traceability and efficient change impact analysis."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "req_3_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA processes a change request that modifies a previously approved and baselined requirement", answer: "Process: (1) Receive the change request formally (not verbally—require it in writing), (2) Assign a Change Request ID, (3) Perform impact analysis using the RTM to identify affected user stories, test cases, and timeline, (4) Document the options and trade-offs, (5) Route to the appropriate decision-maker (per DACI), (6) If approved: update the requirements document with a new version number, log the change, and notify the development team of the updated requirement, (7) If rejected: document the rejection and rationale. Never make undocumented changes to a baselined requirement.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Section 5: User Stories (21 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "user_stories",
            title: "User Stories",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.47, blue: 0.96),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.95, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "text.book.closed.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "us_obj_1",
                    title: "Understand the components of a user story to perform thorough analysis.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_1", number: 1, question: "Identifying the difference between an epic, a feature, and a user story in an agile backlog hierarchy", answer: "Epic: a large body of work representing a major capability or business objective—too large to complete in a single sprint (e.g., 'Implement Lead Management'). Feature: a meaningful chunk of an epic that delivers a distinct piece of functionality (e.g., 'Lead Assignment Rules'). User story: a small, sprint-sized unit of work that delivers a specific piece of value to a specific user (e.g., 'As a Sales Rep, I want new Leads automatically assigned to me based on territory so that I don't miss inbound opportunities'). Epics are broken into features; features are broken into user stories."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_2", number: 2, question: "Identifying the three components of a well-formed user story: role, goal, and benefit (As a… I want… So that…)", answer: "Role (As a...): identifies who the user is—a specific persona, role, or user type. This determines whose needs the story serves. Goal (I want...): describes the specific action or capability the user needs—what they want to do in the system. Benefit (So that...): explains the business value or outcome—why this capability matters. Example: 'As a Sales Manager, I want to see a real-time pipeline dashboard, so that I can identify deals at risk before the end of the quarter.' All three components must be present—a story without 'So that' is missing its justification for prioritization."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_3", number: 3, question: "How a BA creates and uses personas to represent distinct user types, ensuring that user stories cover the full range of end-user needs", answer: "A persona is a fictional but representative character based on research about a user type (e.g., 'Alex, a field sales rep with 5 years experience who works primarily on mobile and manages 50 accounts'). Personas capture: role, technical proficiency, primary goals, pain points, and usage context. The BA creates 3-5 personas covering the distinct user groups in scope. Each persona generates a lens through which user stories are written—ensuring stories reflect real user behaviors and needs rather than abstract system capabilities."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_4", number: 4, question: "How personas inform the 'role' component of user stories and ensure stories reflect real user needs", answer: "When writing 'As a Sales Manager,' the BA draws on the Sales Manager persona to ensure the story reflects that person's actual workflow, technical literacy, and business goal. Without a persona, 'Sales Manager' is vague—does it mean a field manager or an inside sales manager? A regional VP or a team lead? Personas make the role specific and grounded in research. They also reveal when a single story serves multiple user types who have different needs—which may require splitting the story into two distinct stories with separate acceptance criteria."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_5", number: 5, question: "INVEST criteria for a well-written user story (Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable)", answer: "Independent: the story can be developed without depending on another story being completed first. Negotiable: the details of the story are open to discussion—it is not a rigid contract. Valuable: it delivers value to the user or business—not a purely technical task. Estimable: the development team can estimate its size—if they cannot, it needs more clarity or decomposition. Small: fits within a single sprint—usually 1-3 days of development work. Testable: clear acceptance criteria exist that can be verified. A story failing any INVEST criterion should be revised before entering a sprint."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_6", number: 6, question: "Evaluating whether a given user story is properly formed or missing a key component", answer: "Check each component: Does it have a clear role (As a...)? Is the role specific enough to be meaningful? Does it have a goal (I want...)? Is the goal a system action, not a business outcome? Does it have a benefit (So that...)? Is the benefit a real business value, not just restating the goal? Then apply INVEST: Can it be developed independently? Is it small enough for one sprint? Does it have testable acceptance criteria? If any check fails, the story needs revision before it is sprint-ready. The BA is responsible for catching these gaps during backlog grooming."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_7", number: 7, question: "Splitting an overly large epic into individual user stories that are small enough to fit within a single sprint", answer: "Common splitting techniques: (1) By workflow step—one story per step in the process, (2) By user type—separate stories for each persona, (3) By data variation—one story for simple cases, another for complex edge cases, (4) By CRUD operations—separate stories for Create, Read, Update, Delete, (5) By priority—a thin end-to-end slice first, then enhancements. Avoid splitting by technical layer (front-end vs. back-end)—stories should deliver user-visible value. Each resulting story must still pass INVEST criteria."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_8", number: 8, question: "Distinguishing the Definition of Ready (DoR) from the Definition of Done (DoD) and why both must be defined before a sprint begins", answer: "Definition of Ready (DoR): criteria a user story must meet before it can be pulled into sprint planning. Common criteria: story is written in correct format, acceptance criteria are defined and agreed, story is estimated, dependencies are identified and resolved, UX designs are attached if needed. Definition of Done (DoD): criteria that must be true for a story to be considered complete. Common criteria: code is built and reviewed, unit tests pass, acceptance criteria are verified in the QA environment, story is demonstrated to the BA or PO. Both must be defined upfront so the team has a shared standard for quality and readiness."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_9", number: 9, question: "How a BA participates in backlog refinement/grooming to ensure user stories are ready for sprint planning (well-formed, sized, prioritized)", answer: "During backlog grooming (a regular session before sprint planning), the BA: reviews stories at the top of the backlog, verifies each meets the Definition of Ready, clarifies ambiguous acceptance criteria with input from developers and QA, helps the team estimate stories by answering questions about scope and edge cases, and works with the Product Owner to confirm prioritization reflects current business value. Stories that are not ready are revised or parked. The goal: sprint planning is fast and focused because the backlog is already well-prepared."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_1_10", number: 10, question: "The BA's responsibilities during sprint ceremonies: sprint planning, daily standup, sprint review, and retrospective", answer: "Sprint planning: clarify story details, confirm acceptance criteria, answer developer questions. Daily standup: flag any blockers related to requirements clarity or stakeholder availability—the BA does not report on technical progress but identifies BA-specific impediments. Sprint review: evaluate the demonstrated increment against acceptance criteria—the BA confirms whether the story meets the agreed conditions or identifies gaps. Retrospective: reflect on BA process effectiveness—were requirements clear? Were stories well-formed? Contribute improvement suggestions for the BA process in the next sprint.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "us_obj_2",
                    title: "Demonstrate how to write acceptance criteria that convey the requirements and are ready for development.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_1", number: 1, question: "Definition of Done (DoD) versus acceptance criteria: understanding their distinct roles in story completion", answer: "Acceptance criteria are specific to an individual user story—they define the conditions under which that story is considered complete from the business perspective. Example: 'Given a closed-won opportunity, when the stage is updated, then an email is sent to the Account Owner.' The Definition of Done is team-wide—it defines the quality standards all stories must meet regardless of content (code reviewed, tests passing, deployed to QA). A story is 'done' only when both its specific acceptance criteria are met AND the team's DoD is satisfied."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_2", number: 2, question: "How acceptance criteria differ from test cases: acceptance criteria define the conditions of satisfaction; test cases define the specific steps to verify those conditions", answer: "Acceptance criteria (BA responsibility): 'Given a Lead with a valid email, when the Lead is converted, then a Contact is created with the same email address.' This states the business condition that must be true. Test case (QA responsibility): 'Step 1: Create a Lead record with email test@example.com. Step 2: Click Convert Lead. Step 3: Verify Contact record is created. Step 4: Verify Contact email = test@example.com.' The test case operationalizes the acceptance criterion into executable steps. The BA writes acceptance criteria; QA writes test cases from them."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_3", number: 3, question: "Writing acceptance criteria in Given/When/Then (Gherkin) format for a user story", answer: "Gherkin format structures acceptance criteria as: Given [context or precondition], When [user action or system event], Then [expected outcome]. Example for a Lead assignment story: 'Given a new Lead is created with a zip code in the Southwest territory, When the Lead is saved, Then the Lead Owner is automatically set to the Southwest Territory Manager.' Benefits: unambiguous, directly translatable to automated test scripts, easy for business users to read, and ensures all three parts of a scenario (setup, trigger, result) are defined."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_4", number: 4, question: "Difference between functional acceptance criteria and non-functional acceptance criteria within a user story", answer: "Functional acceptance criteria verify what the system does: 'Given a submitted case, when the priority is High, then an email alert is sent to the Case Owner within 1 minute.' Non-functional acceptance criteria verify how the system performs: 'The case list view must load in under 2 seconds for a queue containing up to 500 cases' (performance), or 'Only users with the Service Manager profile can edit the Priority field' (security). Both types should be included in a user story's acceptance criteria—not just the functional behavior."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_5", number: 5, question: "Evaluating whether a set of acceptance criteria is complete, testable, and unambiguous before a story enters development", answer: "Complete: covers all positive scenarios (happy path), negative scenarios (invalid inputs, error states), and edge cases. Testable: each criterion can be verified with a specific, executable test—avoid language like 'the system works correctly' or 'the user experience is smooth.' Unambiguous: only one interpretation is possible—avoid relative terms like 'fast,' 'recent,' or 'few.' Test by asking: 'Can I write a test case for this?' and 'Could two people read this and interpret it differently?' If yes to the latter, rewrite."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_6", number: 6, question: "Identifying acceptance criteria that are too vague to be tested and rewriting them to be specific and verifiable", answer: "Vague: 'The dashboard should be easy to use.' Rewritten: 'A new user can navigate to the Opportunity Dashboard and filter by date range within 2 clicks from the home page, without training.' Vague: 'The system should respond quickly.' Rewritten: 'Report results must display within 3 seconds for datasets up to 10,000 records.' Vague: 'Managers should have more access.' Rewritten: 'Users with the Sales Manager profile can view, edit, and delete all Opportunity records in their region regardless of record ownership.' Specificity is the BA's tool for eliminating vagueness."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_2_7", number: 7, question: "How a BA collaborates with a developer and QA to refine acceptance criteria before sprint planning", answer: "This is often called a Three Amigos session: the BA (requirements perspective), developer (implementation perspective), and QA (testing perspective) review each story together before sprint planning. The developer asks: 'What should happen if the user submits without filling in a required field?' The QA asks: 'How do I test the negative path?' The BA refines or adds acceptance criteria based on these questions. Three Amigos conversations surface gaps before development begins, when they are cheapest to fix, rather than mid-sprint or during UAT.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "us_obj_3",
                    title: "Document user stories in a version controlled repository to manage scope.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_3_1", number: 1, question: "How documenting user stories in a version-controlled repository supports traceability and prevents uncontrolled scope changes", answer: "When user stories are stored in a version-controlled tool (Jira, Azure DevOps, Salesforce's Agility or similar), every change to a story is logged with who made it and when. This prevents quiet scope additions—if a developer expands a story's acceptance criteria without BA approval, the change is visible in the history. The BA can compare the current story against the originally approved version and flag unauthorized scope changes. Traceability is maintained by linking stories to requirements IDs and epics."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_3_2", number: 2, question: "Best practices for organizing user stories in a backlog tool or repository (labels, epics, linking to requirements)", answer: "Best practices: (1) Link every story to its parent epic and feature, (2) Tag stories with labels for the functional area, sprint, and phase, (3) Link each story to the corresponding requirement ID in the RTM, (4) Use a consistent naming convention (e.g., '[Epic]: [Story description]'), (5) Include a 'dependencies' field linking to any stories that must be completed first, (6) Attach wireframes, process maps, or data dictionaries as story attachments. A well-organized backlog makes it easy for the development team to find context and for the BA to maintain traceability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_3_3", number: 3, question: "Using version history of user stories to audit scope changes and justify decisions to project stakeholders", answer: "When a stakeholder asks 'Why was this feature cut?' or 'What happened to the mobile requirement?' the BA can pull the version history of the relevant story to show: when the change was made, who made it, and what the original vs. current acceptance criteria were. This transparency prevents disputes based on faulty memory and provides an objective audit trail for post-project reviews. It also supports lessons-learned analysis by showing patterns in how scope changed over the project lifecycle."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "us_3_4", number: 4, question: "How a BA processes a mid-sprint request to add new scope to an agreed user story", answer: "Mid-sprint scope additions are a significant risk to sprint commitments. The BA process: (1) Do not accept the change verbally—require it in writing, (2) Assess the impact: does the addition change the story's size estimate? Does it affect the sprint commitment? (3) Present options to the Product Owner and Scrum Master: defer the addition to a new story in the next sprint (preferred), or accept it and drop another story to preserve sprint capacity, (4) If accepted, update the story with a new version, log the change, and inform the team. Never silently expand a story that has already been estimated.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),
        // Section 6: Development Support and User Acceptance (18 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "development_support_and_user_acceptance",
            title: "Development Support and User Acceptance",
            color: Color(red: 0.83, green: 0.18, blue: 0.18),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.93, blue: 0.93),
            icon: "checkmark.seal.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "dua_obj_1",
                    title: "Support and validate project delivery to ensure the solution meets the requirements.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_1", number: 1, question: "The BA's responsibilities during the development phase: clarifying requirements, participating in demos, and managing change requests", answer: "During development, the BA is on call for the team. Clarifying requirements: when a developer hits an ambiguous acceptance criterion, the BA is the first point of contact—not the stakeholder directly. The BA interprets the requirement, confirms with stakeholders if needed, and updates the story. Participating in demos: the BA reviews working builds against acceptance criteria in sprint reviews. Managing change requests: the BA logs, assesses, and routes any scope additions that arrive during the build phase through formal change control."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_2", number: 2, question: "The BA's role in the sprint review ceremony: evaluating the demonstrated increment against acceptance criteria and providing feedback to the development team", answer: "At sprint review, the development team demonstrates completed user stories. The BA's role: review each story against its acceptance criteria ('Does this behavior match what we agreed?'), test edge cases if possible, and provide clear pass/fail feedback. If a story does not fully meet its acceptance criteria, the BA documents the specific gaps as defects—not as new requirements. The BA also facilitates stakeholder feedback, helping stakeholders articulate what they see in terms of requirements rather than generating open-ended new requests."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_3", number: 3, question: "Responding to a developer's question about a requirement that has ambiguous acceptance criteria during the sprint", answer: "When a developer asks for clarification, the BA should: (1) Not give an immediate verbal answer if the question involves a trade-off or design decision—check the requirement documentation first, (2) Consult the relevant stakeholder if the question reveals a genuine gap, (3) Provide written clarification (update the story or add a comment) rather than only verbal direction, (4) If the clarification represents a scope change, create a new story rather than expanding the current one, (5) Document the resolution so it can be referenced if the same question arises in testing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_4", number: 4, question: "How a BA tracks and documents defects or gaps discovered during development demo reviews", answer: "During demo reviews, the BA maintains a defect log. For each issue found: (1) Document the specific behavior observed vs. the expected behavior per acceptance criteria, (2) Assign a severity (blocker, major, minor), (3) Link it to the specific user story and acceptance criterion it violates, (4) Assign it to the appropriate developer for remediation, (5) Confirm it is retested and resolved before the story is marked Done. The defect log distinguishes between genuine defects (behavior does not match agreed criteria) and change requests (stakeholder wants something new)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_5", number: 5, question: "How a BA validates that a completed build meets the original acceptance criteria before UAT begins", answer: "Before handing off to UAT, the BA conducts a pre-UAT validation check: (1) Review all user stories marked as complete against their acceptance criteria, (2) Confirm each story has been tested by QA and defects are resolved, (3) Verify the requirements traceability matrix shows 100% of requirements are covered by delivered stories, (4) Spot-check key scenarios in the QA environment, (5) Confirm non-functional requirements (performance, security) have been verified. This gate prevents business users from encountering obvious defects in UAT, which damages trust and wastes testing time."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_6", number: 6, question: "How a BA uses a requirements traceability matrix to confirm all requirements are addressed by the delivered solution", answer: "The BA reviews the RTM column by column: every requirement should have a linked user story, every user story should have a 'Done' status, and every story should have a linked and passed test case. Requirements without linked stories represent coverage gaps that must be addressed before UAT. Requirements with stories in 'In Progress' status are not yet ready for UAT. The RTM gives the BA a single-pane view of completeness and becomes the evidence package for presenting UAT readiness to the sponsor."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_7", number: 7, question: "Distinguishing between a UAT defect (solution does not meet acceptance criteria) and a change request (stakeholder wants something new), and how the BA routes each", answer: "UAT defect: the delivered solution does not satisfy an agreed acceptance criterion. Example: 'The acceptance criterion says the email should go to the Account Owner, but it is going to the Case Owner.' Route: log as a defect, assign to the dev team for remediation, retest before go-live. Change request: the stakeholder sees the delivered solution and now wants something different or additional that was not in the original acceptance criteria. Example: 'Can we also add the opportunity amount to this email?' Route: log as a change request, assess scope and timeline impact, route through change control. Never treat a change request as a defect."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_8", number: 8, question: "Managing scope creep requests that arrive during the development phase: BA decision-making process", answer: "Scope creep during build is one of the most common project risks. The BA process: (1) Do not accept scope additions verbally—require written submission, (2) Log as a change request with a CR ID, (3) Perform impact analysis: what stories are affected? How much additional effort? What is the timeline impact? (4) Present options to the decision-maker (typically sponsor): accept the change and adjust scope/timeline, defer to Phase 2, or reject, (5) Document the decision. The BA's role is not to approve or reject scope changes—it is to make the impact transparent so the right person can make an informed decision."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_1_9", number: 9, question: "How a BA supports organizational change management activities (training plans, communication, stakeholder readiness) as go-live approaches", answer: "As go-live approaches, the BA shifts from requirements guardian to adoption enabler. Key activities: (1) Contribute to training materials by providing process maps and user story summaries to the training team, (2) Communicate go-live timelines and what is changing to end users through agreed channels, (3) Assess stakeholder readiness—have all intended users been trained? Are there resistance signals that need addressing? (4) Support the creation of quick reference guides based on the processes documented during the project. Note: the BA supports these activities; they do not own the full change management program.")
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "dua_obj_2",
                    title: "Support and validate user acceptance testing (UAT) to ensure the solution meets the requirements.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_1", number: 1, question: "Difference between system testing, integration testing, and UAT — and when each occurs in the implementation lifecycle", answer: "System testing (QA): performed by the development/QA team in a test environment to verify that individual components work correctly according to technical specifications. Occurs after each story is built. Integration testing: verifies that connected systems exchange data correctly—e.g., Salesforce sends the correct payload to the ERP when an Opportunity closes. Occurs after system testing when multiple components are assembled. UAT (User Acceptance Testing): performed by business users to confirm the complete solution meets their business needs. Occurs after integration testing, in a UAT environment, immediately before go-live approval."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_2", number: 2, question: "Distinguishing UAT from pilot testing: UAT validates requirements with selected users in a test environment; a pilot deploys to a limited subset of real users in production", answer: "UAT: occurs in a non-production environment with test data. Selected business users execute scripted test cases to verify the solution meets accepted requirements. Issues found are logged as defects and fixed before go-live. Pilot: occurs in the production environment with real data and real users—a limited rollout (e.g., one region, one team) before full deployment. A pilot tests real-world adoption, performance under production load, and edge cases not covered in UAT. UAT confirms the solution is built correctly; a pilot confirms it works in the real world."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_3", number: 3, question: "The BA's role in planning and coordinating UAT: preparing test scenarios, selecting testers, and scheduling sessions", answer: "The BA leads UAT planning: (1) Identify business users who represent each key user type—testers should be people who know the current process well, (2) Write UAT test scenarios derived from acceptance criteria (not raw acceptance criteria—structured, step-by-step scripts), (3) Schedule sessions with enough time for testers to execute scripts, document results, and retest resolved defects, (4) Confirm the UAT environment is configured and populated with representative test data, (5) Communicate the UAT objectives and tester expectations clearly before sessions begin."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_4", number: 4, question: "Writing UAT test cases derived from acceptance criteria to validate that the solution meets business requirements", answer: "Each UAT test case includes: Test Case ID, related requirement/story ID, test objective (what is being verified), preconditions (what setup is needed), step-by-step test steps, expected result for each step, and pass/fail outcome. Example: Test Case TC-045, Story US-012, Objective: Verify Lead auto-assignment by territory. Precondition: Lead record with zip code 90210 exists. Steps: 1. Save the Lead. 2. Navigate to the Lead record. Expected: Owner field shows 'Southwest Territory Manager.' This level of specificity ensures testers know exactly what to do and what to look for."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_5", number: 5, question: "How a BA prepares end users for UAT: communicating objectives, providing test scripts, and setting expectations about their role", answer: "Before UAT begins, the BA communicates: (1) The purpose of UAT ('You are validating that the system meets the requirements we agreed together—you are not learning how to use it yet'), (2) The tester's role (execute test scripts, document results, report defects—not explore freely), (3) The timeline and commitment required, (4) How to log defects using the agreed tool, (5) What happens with defects found (they are logged, prioritized, and fixed before go-live). Setting expectations prevents testers from treating UAT as training or generating open-ended feature requests."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_6", number: 6, question: "How a BA facilitates UAT sessions to ensure business users test the right scenarios and document results clearly", answer: "During UAT sessions, the BA: (1) Opens each session by reminding testers of objectives and how to log results, (2) Observes and assists testers who are stuck without leading their answers, (3) Captures defects in real time alongside testers, clarifying whether an issue is a defect (vs. a change request) before logging, (4) Manages session time to ensure all test cases are executed, (5) Closes each session with a summary of results and open items. The BA does not test on behalf of users—the goal is for business users to validate the solution themselves."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_7", number: 7, question: "Communicating UAT results (pass/fail rates, open defects, risk assessment) to the project sponsor and stakeholders", answer: "The BA compiles UAT results into a UAT Summary Report covering: total test cases executed, pass rate, number of open defects by severity (blocker, major, minor), risks associated with open defects, and a go/no-go recommendation. Example: '95 of 100 test cases passed. 5 open defects: 0 blockers, 2 major (workaround available), 3 minor. BA recommends go-live with the 2 major defects on the remediation plan for Week 1 post-go-live.' This report enables the sponsor to make an informed go-live decision."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_8", number: 8, question: "Criteria a BA uses to recommend UAT sign-off or withhold approval for go-live", answer: "The BA recommends sign-off when: all Must Have requirements are verified as met, no blocker-severity defects remain open, major defects either have acceptable workarounds or have a confirmed remediation date post-go-live, all key user types have completed UAT, and the business sponsor accepts any remaining risk. The BA withholds sign-off when: any blocker defects are unresolved, Must Have requirements are not fully satisfied, or the defect volume creates unacceptable operational risk. The BA documents the basis for their recommendation regardless of the outcome."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "dua_2_9", number: 9, question: "How a BA handles a UAT defect that stakeholders want to accept as-is to meet a go-live deadline", answer: "When a stakeholder wants to accept a known defect to protect the go-live date, the BA's responsibility is: (1) Ensure the stakeholder fully understands the business impact of the defect—not just the technical description, (2) Confirm whether the defect affects a Must Have requirement—if so, go-live without remediation may breach the original project agreement, (3) Document the risk formally: 'Stakeholder [Name] accepts defect [ID] as-is for go-live. Known impact: [describe]. Remediation plan: [date and owner],' (4) Obtain formal written acceptance of the risk from the decision-maker. The BA does not make this call unilaterally.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        )
    ]
}
